@EnableCaching     // enabling caching in the application
@Cacheable(cacheNames = {"studentCache"}, key = "#studentId") // find existing record
@CachePut(cacheNames = {"studentCache"}, key="#student.studentId")// updating existing record
@CacheEvict(cacheNames = {"studentCache"}, key = "#studentId")// deleting existing record,
    Assume we want to delete an entry from the database as well as from the Cache.
In that case, we can simply use @CacheEvict annotation.

MemCached->
1->Memcached is a high-performance, in-memory key-value store that is
    commonly used as a caching system to speed up web applications.
    It works by storing frequently accessed data in RAM, allowing for faster
    retrieval compared to accessing data from databases or
    other slower storage mediums in memory cached  high-performance memory
    caching system.
2->Memcached is also distributed, meaning that it is easy to scale out by
    adding new nodes.
3->As a result of its speed and scalability as well as its simple design,
    efficient memory management,
    and API support for most popular languages Memcached is a popular choice
    for high-performance, large-scale caching use cases.

scalability:
     Memcached’s distributed and multithreaded architecture makes it easy to scale.
     You can split your data among a number of nodes,
     enabling you to scale out capacity by adding new nodes to your cluster. Moreover,
     since Memcached is multithreaded, it can use multiple cores on a given node.
     This makes it simple to scale up compute capacity. With Memcached you can build
     highly scalable distributed caching solutions designed to provide fast
     and consistent performance.

It’s typically used to cache database data, API calls or page rendering chunks in
     RAM to increase the application performance.
