difference between sharding and replication

Sharding and replication are both database scaling techniques,
but they address different needs.
Sharding distributes data across multiple servers (horizontal scaling) to
handle large datasets and improve query performance,
while replication creates copies of the entire dataset on multiple servers
to enhance availability and fault tolerance.

sharding:
divide data into smaller chunks(shards)
used for scalability and performance improvement

disadvantage
Running queries across multiple shards can be slow and complicated to implement.

replication
copies the same data into multiple servers
used for high availability and fault tolerance
each replicas contains full copies of data

disadvantage
Writes might not instantly reflect on all replicas, leading to inconsistency issues.
Each replica stores the full data, leading to higher storage needs.